R = {
"Irish Famine": ("EP",3,"A potato disease and British policy killed a million Irish and drove a million more abroad.","Blight destroyed the potato, the main food of Irish peasants, year after year. Meanwhile Ireland kept exporting grain and cattle to England, and relief was slow and stingy.","Ireland's population fell by about a quarter and kept shrinking for a century.","Over 30 million Americans claim Irish ancestry; the famine still shapes Irish identity and nationalism."),
"Revolutions of 1848; Communist Manifesto": ("PIW",3,"Revolutions swept Europe in a single spring, and Marx published his call to the workers of the world.","After bad harvests and an economic slump, uprisings broke out in Paris, Vienna, Berlin, Milan and Budapest, demanding constitutions and national unity. Most were crushed within a year. The same year, Marx and Engels published 'The Communist Manifesto'.","Serfdom ended in Austria, and nationalism and socialism became the defining forces of the next century.","Marx's ideas inspired the Russian and Chinese revolutions; '1848' is the template for sudden waves of revolt, like the Arab Spring."),
"Crimean War": ("W",2,"Britain and France fought Russia to keep it from swallowing the Ottoman Empire.","Allied armies besieged Russia's Black Sea base at Sevastopol. Bungling generals and disease killed far more men than bullets. Florence Nightingale reformed military nursing.","Russia was humiliated and began modernizing, including freeing the serfs.","It was the first war covered by telegraph and photographed, the start of modern war reporting."),
"Darwin's Origin of Species": ("SIR",3,"Darwin showed that all life evolved by natural selection.","After decades of research begun on the voyage of the Beagle, Charles Darwin argued that species change over time as the best-adapted survive and reproduce.","It set off fierce debates with religious leaders and transformed biology.","Evolution is the foundation of modern biology and medicine, from vaccines to genetics."),
"Italy unified; Russia frees serfs": ("P",2,"Italy became one country, and Russia freed 23 million serfs, both in 1861.","Count Cavour's diplomacy and Garibaldi's volunteer Redshirts united most of Italy under the King of Piedmont. Tsar Alexander II freed the serfs after losing the Crimean War.","Rome joined Italy in 1870. Russia's peasants stayed poor, which fed later revolution.","Italy's north-south divide dates from unification."),
"Austria-Hungary formed": ("P",2,"Austria shared its empire with Hungary to hold it together.","After losing to Prussia in 1866, Emperor Franz Joseph gave Hungary equal status in a 'dual monarchy' with one ruler and two governments.","The empire's many peoples, Czechs, Slavs, Poles and others, wanted the same.","Its collapse in 1918 created the modern map of Central Europe."),
"Franco-Prussian War": ("W",3,"Prussia crushed France in months and used the victory to create Germany.","Bismarck provoked France into declaring war. Prussian armies with railways and modern artillery captured Napoleon III at Sedan.","Germany was unified; France lost Alsace-Lorraine and wanted revenge.","That desire for revenge was one root of World War I."),
"German Empire; Paris Commune": ("PW",3,"Germany was proclaimed in France's own palace, while Paris rose in a radical revolt.","Wilhelm I became German emperor in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles. In Paris, workers set up the Commune, a radical city government, which French troops crushed, killing thousands.","Germany became Europe's strongest economy and army.","The Commune became a legend for socialists; German power reshaped Europe for 75 years."),
"Congress of Berlin": ("P",2,"Europe's powers redrew the Balkans, leaving everyone unsatisfied.","After Russia beat the Ottomans, Bismarck hosted a congress to limit Russian gains. Serbia, Romania and Montenegro became independent; Austria took over Bosnia.","Serbian anger over Bosnia festered for 36 years.","It set up the tensions that exploded in Sarajevo in 1914."),
"Triple Alliance": ("P",2,"Germany, Austria-Hungary and Italy formed an alliance against France.","Bismarck wanted to keep France isolated. The three powers promised to defend one another.","France answered by allying with Russia, then Britain.","Europe split into two armed camps that went to war in 1914."),
"Berlin Conference": ("PE",3,"European powers met to divide Africa, and invited no Africans.","Bismarck hosted 14 nations to set rules for claiming African territory. A claim needed 'effective occupation', which set off a race to grab land.","By 1914, Europeans controlled about 90% of Africa.","Many of Africa's borders today were drawn by people who had never been there."),
"Franco-Russian Alliance": ("P",2,"France and Russia joined against Germany, creating a two-front threat.","Germany's new Kaiser let the treaty with Russia lapse. France offered loans and friendship.","Germany planned for a two-front war, the Schlieffen Plan.","That plan, to beat France fast before Russia, shaped how WWI began."),
"Germany builds a navy": ("PW",2,"The Kaiser built a battle fleet, and Britain took it as a threat.","Wilhelm II wanted Germany to be a world power like Britain. Admiral Tirpitz's Navy Laws funded a big fleet.","Britain launched the Dreadnought and allied with France and Russia.","An arms race between rival powers is a classic warning sign of war."),
"Entente Cordiale": ("P",2,"Old enemies Britain and France settled their quarrels.","Britain and France agreed on colonial disputes, Egypt for Britain, Morocco for France, out of shared fear of Germany.","Russia joined in 1907, forming the Triple Entente.","Britain and France fought side by side in both world wars."),
"Russian Revolution of 1905": ("PW",2,"Defeat by Japan and a massacre of protesters forced the tsar to share power.","Troops fired on peaceful marchers in St. Petersburg on 'Bloody Sunday'. Strikes and mutinies spread; Nicholas II created a parliament, the Duma.","He soon ignored it, and the anger remained.","Lenin called it the 'dress rehearsal' for 1917."),
"WWI begins": ("W",3,"An assassination in Sarajevo dragged Europe's empires into a catastrophic war.","A Bosnian Serb shot Austria's Archduke Franz Ferdinand. Within weeks, alliances pulled in Russia, Germany, France and Britain. Machine guns and trenches made it a war of slaughter.","About 20 million died, and four empires collapsed.","The map of the Middle East, Eastern Europe, and the causes of WWII all came from this war."),
"Russian Revolution": ("PIW",3,"Russia overthrew its tsar, and Lenin's Bolsheviks seized power.","War losses, hunger and strikes forced Nicholas II to abdicate in February 1917. In October, Lenin's Bolsheviks took power promising 'peace, land and bread'. A bloody civil war followed.","The USSR became the world's first communist state.","The 20th century's great ideological struggle, communism vs. capitalism, starts here."),
"Armistice; empires collapse": ("WP",3,"At 11 a.m. on 11 November, the guns stopped, and four empires were gone.","Germany, exhausted and in revolution, signed an armistice in a railway car in France.","The German, Austro-Hungarian, Russian and Ottoman empires fell, creating new countries like Poland, Czechoslovakia and Yugoslavia.","Remembrance Day is marked at 11 a.m. on November 11."),
"Treaty of Versailles": ("P",3,"A harsh peace punished Germany, and planted the seeds of the next war.","The victors blamed Germany for the war, took its colonies and some land, limited its army, and demanded huge reparations.","Bitter Germans called it a 'dictated peace'; Hitler exploited that anger.","It's the classic warning about humiliating a defeated enemy."),
"Mussolini takes power; USSR formed": ("PI",3,"Fascism took power in Italy as the Soviet Union was founded.","Mussolini's Blackshirts marched on Rome and the king made him prime minister. In Moscow, Russia and neighboring republics formed the USSR.","Italy became the first fascist state and the model for Hitler.","The word 'fascist' is still the sharpest political insult."),
"Stalin rises": ("P",3,"Stalin outmaneuvered rivals and became the Soviet Union's dictator.","After Lenin's death, Stalin used his control of the party to push aside Trotsky and others.","Forced collectivization, famine and purges killed millions; the USSR also industrialized fast.","Ukraine's 1932–33 famine, the Holodomor, is remembered as a national tragedy."),
"Hitler takes power": ("P",3,"Adolf Hitler became chancellor of Germany and built a dictatorship within months.","The Depression and anger at Versailles boosted the Nazi Party. Conservatives made Hitler chancellor, thinking they could control him. A fire at the Reichstag gave him an excuse to crush opponents.","Germany rearmed and persecuted Jews; it led directly to WWII and the Holocaust.","It's the standard example of how a democracy can be dismantled from within."),
"Spanish Civil War": ("W",2,"Spain's civil war became a dress rehearsal for WWII.","General Franco's Nationalists rose against the elected Republic. Germany and Italy backed Franco; the USSR backed the Republic; volunteers came from everywhere.","Franco won and ruled until 1975; German bombers tested tactics on Guernica.","Picasso's 'Guernica' became the world's most famous anti-war painting."),
"Munich Agreement": ("P",2,"Britain and France gave Hitler part of Czechoslovakia to avoid war.","Chamberlain and Daladier agreed to let Germany take the Sudetenland without asking the Czechs. Chamberlain promised 'peace for our time'.","Hitler took the rest of Czechoslovakia six months later and invaded Poland after that.","'Munich' became shorthand for appeasing aggressors."),
"WWII begins": ("W",3,"Germany invaded Poland, and the deadliest war in history began.","Hitler, having made a secret deal with Stalin to split Poland, invaded on September 1, 1939. Britain and France declared war two days later.","70–85 million people died, including 6 million Jews in the Holocaust.","The postwar world, the UN, NATO, the Cold War, decolonization, grew from its ruins."),
"Fall of France": ("W",2,"France collapsed in six weeks.","German tanks raced through the Ardennes forest, avoiding France's defensive Maginot Line. British troops evacuated at Dunkirk.","Britain, under Churchill, fought on alone for a year.","Charles de Gaulle's Free French movement began here."),
"Germany invades USSR": ("W",3,"Hitler launched the largest invasion in history, and it decided the war.","Over 3 million Axis troops invaded the Soviet Union. They reached Moscow's outskirts before winter and Soviet counterattacks stopped them.","Stalingrad (1942–43) turned the tide; the USSR lost about 27 million people.","Russia remembers it as the 'Great Patriotic War'; Victory Day is its biggest holiday."),
"D-Day": ("W",3,"The largest seaborne invasion ever opened the Western front.","On June 6, 1944, about 156,000 Allied troops landed on five beaches in Normandy.","Paris was freed in August; Germany was squeezed between East and West.","D-Day anniversaries are major moments of Western alliance remembrance."),
"Germany surrenders; Holocaust revealed": ("WPR",3,"Germany surrendered, and the world saw the full horror of the Holocaust.","As Allied troops reached the camps, they found survivors and mass graves. The Nazis had murdered about 6 million Jews and millions of others. Hitler killed himself as Berlin fell.","The Nuremberg trials prosecuted Nazi leaders for crimes against humanity.","The Genocide Convention and Universal Declaration of Human Rights followed."),
"NATO founded; Germany divided": ("P",3,"The West formed a military alliance, and Germany split in two.","After the Soviet blockade of Berlin, the US, Canada and Western Europe signed NATO: an attack on one is an attack on all. West and East Germany were created.","The Soviets formed the Warsaw Pact in 1955.","NATO has grown to 32 members, with Finland and Sweden joining after Russia's 2022 invasion of Ukraine."),
"Stalin dies": ("P",2,"Stalin's death opened a thaw in the Soviet Union.","Stalin died after a stroke. Nikita Khrushchev rose to power and in 1956 denounced Stalin's crimes in a secret speech.","Millions were released from the Gulag labor camps.","Russians are still divided over Stalin's legacy."),
"Hungarian uprising crushed": ("WP",2,"Hungarians rose up against Soviet rule, and Soviet tanks crushed them.","Students and workers demanded freedom and a neutral Hungary. The USSR invaded; about 2,500 Hungarians were killed and 200,000 fled.","The West did nothing, showing it would not risk war over Eastern Europe.","Hungary commemorates October 23 as a national holiday."),
"Treaty of Rome (EEC)": ("PE",3,"Six countries started the common market that became the European Union.","France, West Germany, Italy and the Benelux countries created the European Economic Community. The idea: link economies so war between them becomes impossible.","It grew into the EU with 27 members.","European countries that fought three wars in 70 years haven't fought each other since."),
"Berlin Wall built": ("P",2,"East Germany sealed off West Berlin with a wall overnight.","About 3 million East Germans had fled West through Berlin. On August 13, 1961, the government strung barbed wire, then built a concrete wall.","At least 140 people died trying to cross it.","It became the symbol of the Cold War."),
"Prague Spring; Paris protests": ("PIC",2,"1968: Czechs tried 'socialism with a human face', and students shook Paris.","Alexander Dubček eased censorship in Czechoslovakia until Soviet tanks invaded in August. In Paris, student protests sparked a general strike of 10 million workers.","The Soviet bloc hardened; in the West, a cultural revolution in attitudes followed.","'1968' still stands for youth rebellion."),
"Helsinki Accords; Franco dies": ("PI",2,"Cold War rivals pledged to respect human rights, and Spain began its path to democracy.","The USSR got recognition of postwar borders; in return it signed human rights promises. Franco's death let Spain become a democracy.","Dissidents in the East used Helsinki's promises to challenge their governments.","Spain's peaceful transition became a model for other dictatorships."),
"Solidarity in Poland": ("P",2,"Polish shipyard workers created the Soviet bloc's first free trade union.","Strikes led by electrician Lech Wałęsa in Gdańsk forced the government to accept Solidarity, which grew to 10 million members.","Martial law crushed it in 1981, but it returned to win elections in 1989.","Wałęsa won the Nobel Peace Prize and became Poland's president."),
"Gorbachev comes to power": ("P",3,"A reformer took over the Soviet Union and accidentally helped end it.","Mikhail Gorbachev introduced glasnost (openness) and perestroika (restructuring) to fix a stagnant economy. He refused to use force to hold Eastern Europe.","Within six years communism collapsed in Europe and the USSR dissolved.","He's admired in the West and widely blamed in Russia."),
"Chernobyl disaster": ("SP",2,"The world's worst nuclear accident exposed the Soviet system's lies.","A botched safety test blew apart Reactor 4 in Ukraine. Soviet officials hid the scale for days while radiation spread across Europe.","About 350,000 people were evacuated; a 30-km exclusion zone remains.","It still shapes debates about nuclear power."),
"Berlin Wall falls": ("P",3,"Crowds poured through the Berlin Wall, and the Cold War started to end.","After mass protests, East Germany loosened travel rules; a spokesman mistakenly said they took effect 'immediately'. Thousands rushed the checkpoints and guards let them through.","Communist governments fell across Eastern Europe within weeks.","November 9 became a symbol of peaceful revolution."),
"Germany reunified": ("P",2,"East and West Germany became one country again after 41 years.","After the Wall fell, East Germany's government collapsed. Helmut Kohl negotiated reunification with the four World War II victors.","Germany became Europe's largest economy and leading power.","Gaps in wages and politics between East and West persist."),
"USSR dissolves; Yugoslav wars begin": ("PW",3,"The Soviet Union broke into 15 countries, and Yugoslavia broke apart in blood.","A failed hard-line coup in August 1991 weakened Gorbachev; republics declared independence and the USSR ended on December 25. In Yugoslavia, nationalist leaders led Slovenia, Croatia and Bosnia into war.","The Cold War ended; about 140,000 died in the Yugoslav wars.","Ukraine, the Baltics and Central Asia's states became independent."),
"Maastricht Treaty creates EU": ("PE",2,"The European Union was born, with a plan for one currency.","Twelve countries agreed to closer political ties, common citizenship and a single currency.","The euro launched in 1999.","The EU is today one of the world's largest economies."),
"Srebrenica massacre": ("W",2,"Bosnian Serb forces murdered about 8,000 Muslim men and boys in a UN 'safe area'.","Dutch UN peacekeepers were overwhelmed as Ratko Mladić's troops took the town. Men were separated and executed.","NATO bombing and the Dayton Accords ended the war that year.","An international court ruled it genocide; Mladić was jailed for life."),
"Euro notes & coins": ("E",2,"Twelve countries swapped their old currencies for the euro.","Marks, francs and lire disappeared as the euro entered daily use.","The shared currency made trade easier but locked weak and strong economies together.","About 350 million people now use the euro."),
"EU expands east": ("P",2,"Ten countries, mostly former communist states, joined the EU in one day.","Poland, the Czech Republic, Hungary, the Baltic states and others joined.","Millions moved west for work; Russia saw it as encroachment.","It's the biggest enlargement in EU history."),
"Russia-Georgia war": ("W",2,"Russia fought a five-day war with Georgia, a preview of Ukraine.","Georgia tried to retake the breakaway region of South Ossetia; Russia invaded and recognized two breakaway regions as independent.","The West's response was mild.","Many see it as the first sign of Russia's willingness to redraw borders by force."),
"Eurozone debt crisis": ("E",2,"Greece nearly went bankrupt and the euro almost broke apart.","Greece revealed huge hidden deficits; bond markets panicked about Portugal, Ireland, Spain and Italy too. The EU and IMF offered bailouts in exchange for harsh austerity.","Greek unemployment passed 25%.","Anti-EU populism surged across Europe."),
"Russia annexes Crimea": ("WP",3,"Russia seized Crimea from Ukraine, the first annexation in Europe since WWII.","After Ukraine's pro-EU protests ousted its president, Russian troops took Crimea and held a disputed referendum. War broke out in the Donbas.","The West imposed sanctions.","It was the prelude to the full-scale invasion of 2022."),
"Migrant crisis": ("P",2,"Over a million refugees reached Europe in one year.","Most fled Syria's war, crossing from Turkey to Greece and up through the Balkans. Germany took in nearly a million.","Border fences went up; the EU paid Turkey to stop crossings.","Migration became the hottest issue in European politics."),
"Brexit vote": ("P",2,"Britain voted to leave the European Union.","Campaigners promised to 'take back control' of laws, money and borders. Leave won 52% to 48%.","Four years of political chaos and negotiations followed.","Britain is the only country ever to leave the EU."),
"Brexit takes effect": ("PE",1,"Britain formally left the European Union.","After years of negotiation, the UK left on January 31, 2020, and the transition ended in 2021.","New checks hit trade with Europe and Northern Ireland.","The UK and EU are still adjusting their relationship."),
"Russia invades Ukraine": ("W",3,"Russia launched the largest war in Europe since 1945.","Putin's forces invaded from three sides, expecting to take Kyiv in days. Ukraine held, then pushed Russia back from the north and around Kherson. The war turned into grinding battles in the east.","Hundreds of thousands have been killed or wounded; Finland and Sweden joined NATO.","It reshaped European security, energy and defense spending."),
}
